-- title:
--   Divisor locality on the disc at a smooth point
-- statement:
--   Let $k_0 \subseteq L \subseteq F$ be fields forming a scalar tower, with $L$ of characteristic zero and algebraically closed and `IsCurveOver L F` (so $F/L$ has principal divisors, every place has residue field finite over $L$, and $\Omega_{F/L}$ is free of rank one over $F$); let $F_0$ be a $k_0$-subfield of $F$ with $k_0(\operatorname{im}L) \sqcup F_0 = F$, let $A$ be a valuation subring of $L$, $\bar F$ an extension of the residue field of $A$, and $R$ a regular prolongation of $A$ to $F$ with residue map to $\bar F$. Fix a layer: an intermediate field $K_1$ of $L/k_0$ with $L/K_1$ algebraic, a valuation subring $A_1 \subseteq K_1$ cut out by $A$, with residues of $A_1$ surjecting onto the residue field of $A$; a subring $S_1 \subseteq F$ together with $\varphi_1 : A_1[X] \to S_1$, $\chi_1 : S_1 \to \kappa(A)$ and a set $D_1$ of places of $F/L$. The hypotheses, summarised here, say: $\varphi_1$ sends constants $a$ to the image of $a$ in $F$ and $\chi_1 \circ \varphi_1$ computes residues of constants, $\chi_1(\varphi_1 X) = 0$, and $\varphi_1 X$ is not a constant; $D_1$ consists exactly of the rational places $P$ at which every $f \in S_1$ is regular with $P$-value in $A$ and at which the value of $f$ is in the maximal ideal of $A$ precisely when $\chi_1 f = 0$; $S_1$ is a Noetherian local unique factorisation domain whose maximal ideal is the kernel of $\chi_1$; writing $F_1 = k_0(\operatorname{im}K_1) \sqcup F_0$, one has $S_1 \subseteq F_1$ and every element of $F_1$ is a quotient of elements of $S_1$, while $F_1$ is linearly disjoint from $L$ over $K_1$ in the sense that $K_1$-linearly independent tuples in $L$ remain $F_1$-linearly independent; every proper valuation subring of $F$ containing $L$ is a principal ideal ring; $\varpi$ generates the maximal ideal of $A_1$, is nonzero, and $\varphi_1(\varpi)$ is prime in $S_1$; $R \cap F_1$ is the localisation of $S_1$ at $\varphi_1(\varpi)$; any ring homomorphism $S_1 \to A_1$ splitting the constants and compatible with $\chi_1$ has kernel generated by $\varphi_1 X - \varphi_1(C(\chi(\varphi_1 X)))$; and for every prime $p$ of $S_1$ not associated to $\varphi_1(\varpi)$ and every $x \in S_1$ there is a monic $r \in A_1[X]$ with $p$ dividing $r(x)$ computed via $\varphi_1 \circ C$. The conclusion has two parts: first, any nonzero $f \in F_1$ with $\operatorname{ord}_P f = 0$ for all $P \in D_1$ satisfies $c f = u$ for some nonzero $c \in K_1$ and some unit $u$ of $S_1$; second, any $f \in F_1$ lying in $R$ and regular at every $P \in D_1$ lies in $S_1$.
--
--   This is the locality statement for the disc cut out by a smooth point: functions with neither zeros nor poles on the disc $D_1$ are units of the local ring $S_1$ up to a constant factor, and functions integral for the prolongation $R$ and regular on $D_1$ belong to $S_1$. It is used in the assembly of the smooth-point frame for curves, namely in [`AlgebraicCurve.RegularProlongation.disc_sections_locality_of_smoothPoint`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.RegularProlongation.disc_sections_locality_of_smoothPoint), and rests on the two dictionaries between primes of $S_1$ and places in the disc.
